#!lm
MODEL=litellm_proxy/anthropic/Claude Opus 5
CONTEXT=urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:1.1.t0-urn:cts:latinLit:phi0474.phi016.omar:1.1.t71
REASONING=Structure: three coordinated conditional protases ("si quid est...", "aut si qua exercitatio dicendi [est]...", "aut si huiusce rei ratio aliqua ... profecta [est]"), each with its own relative clause, all subordinate to the main clause "hic A. Licinius fructum a me repetere prope suo iure debet." - 1.t2 "est" is the existential verb of the first protasis (dependent on "si" 1.t0, which subordinates to "debet"); "quid" is its subject, "ingeni" a partitive genitive depending on "quid", "in me" adverbial. - "quod sentio quam sit exiguum": "quod" is a relative pronoun whose antecedent is "quid" (1.t1) and is at the same time the proleptic direct object of "sentio" (slot 2). "sentio" is the dependent verb of that relative clause; "quam" introduces an indirect question and is treated like a subordinating conjunction (-> "sentio"), with "sit" (linking) as its dependent verb and "exiguum" as predicate. - Second protasis has no expressed verb ("aut si qua exercitatio dicendi"): an elided present of *sum*, so an 'implied sum' token (1.t19_implied) anchors a dependent verbal expression; "exercitatio" is its subject, "qua" (indefinite) adjectival, "dicendi" a gerund in the genitive depending on "exercitatio". Since the construction is existential rather than subject+predicate-noun, I classified it 'intransitive', matching 1.t2 "est". - "in qua me non infitior mediocriter esse versatum": "qua" is relative (antecedent "exercitatio") and object of the preposition "in"; "infitior" is the relative clause's dependent verb; "versatum (esse)" anchors the indirect statement (participle anchors, "esse" is its auxiliary), with "me" as accusative subject. - Third protasis: "profecta" with omitted auxiliary = compound perfect (deponent *proficiscor*), so "profecta" itself anchors the dependent verbal expression and an 'implied sum' token (1.t42_implied) relates to it as auxiliary; "ratio" is subject, "ab ... studiis ac disciplina" adverbial with "ac" coordinating the two ablative objects. - "a qua ego nullum confiteor aetatis meae tempus abhorruisse": "confiteor" is the relative clause's verb, "abhorruisse" the governed indirect-statement infinitive with "tempus" as its accusative subject; "a" (1.t44) is adverbial to "abhorruisse" (source, not agent — the verb is active). - Main clause: "debet" (independent, transitive active) with complementary infinitive "repetere"; "fructum" is "repetere"'s object, "earum rerum omnium" genitive depending on "fructum", "a me" adverbial to "repetere", "suo iure" ablative of manner with "repetere". Judgment calls flagged: - Antecedent of "a qua" (1.t45): it could be "ratio" (1.t34) or the nearer "disciplina" (1.t41); I chose "disciplina" as the nearest feminine singular and the more natural source for "abhorrere a", but "ratio" is defensible. - "iudices" (1.t7) stands physically inside the first si-clause but addresses the whole sentence; I related it to the main verb "debet" rather than to "est". - The adverbs "vel" (1.t57) and "prope" (1.t67) intensify a phrase rather than a verb; I attached "vel" to "in" (1.t58, head of "in primis") and "prope" to "iure" (1.t69), rather than to the main verb. - "quod" (1.t9) as proleptic object of "sentio" vs. subject of "sit": I took the proleptic-object reading (its relative-pronoun slot is already used for the antecedent link, so no further slot is available for a subject link to "sit").

